The Repository pattern follows the Dependency Inversion Principle: high-level modules (service) depend on abstractions (repository interface), not low-level modules (JPA implementation). The service never imports JPA, EntityManager, or SQL — it only knows the repository interface.

```java
// ═══ REPOSITORY INTERFACE — defines WHAT, not HOW ═══
public interface TradeRepository {
    List<Trade> findSettledByAccountId(String accountId);
    Optional<Trade> findById(String tradeId);
    Trade save(Trade trade);
    void deleteById(String tradeId);
}
// Service depends on THIS interface. Doesn't know about JPA, SQL, or MongoDB.

// ═══ JPA IMPLEMENTATION (Spring Data generates this!) ═══
public interface JpaTradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {
    List<Trade> findByAccountIdAndStatus(String accountId, String status);
}
// Spring Data auto-generates the implementation class at runtime.

// ═══ SERVICE — depends on INTERFACE, not JPA ═══
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class SettlementService {
    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;  // interface — not JPA-specific!

    public BigDecimal calculateSettledTotal(String accountId) {
        return tradeRepo.findSettledByAccountId(accountId).stream()
            .map(Trade::getAmount)
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}
```

Comparison: Repository vs DAO. Both abstract data access. DAO is more generic and traditionally exposes CRUD at a lower level. Repository is DDD-oriented (Domain-Driven Design) — it acts as a collection of domain objects. In practice with Spring Data, the distinction blurs because `JpaRepository` is both a repository AND provides DAO-level CRUD.

```java
// ═══ DAO (traditional — lower-level, SQL visible) ═══
public class TradeDao {
    @Autowired private JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    public List<Trade> findSettled(String accountId) {
        return jdbc.query("SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id = ? AND status = 'SETTLED'",
            new TradeRowMapper(), accountId);
        // SQL is visible in the DAO. DAO knows about JDBC/SQL.
    }
}

// ═══ REPOSITORY (Spring Data — higher-level, no SQL) ═══
public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {
    List<Trade> findByAccountIdAndStatus(String accountId, String status);
    // No SQL! Spring generates the query from the method name.
    // Implementation auto-generated at runtime.
}
```

Why keep queries OUT of controllers and services. Controllers handle HTTP concerns (request parsing, response formatting). Services handle business logic (calculate total, apply rules). Repositories handle data access (queries, persistence). If a controller contains SQL or JPQL, it violates single responsibility — changing the database requires changing the controller.

```java
// (Avoid) BAD: query in controller
@GetMapping("/trades")
public List<Trade> getTrades(@RequestParam String accountId) {
    return em.createQuery("SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.accountId = :id", Trade.class)
        .setParameter("id", accountId).getResultList();
    // Controller knows about EntityManager + JPQL — tight coupling!
}

// (Recommended) GOOD: query in repository
@GetMapping("/trades")
public List<Trade> getTrades(@RequestParam String accountId) {
    return tradeService.getTradesForAccount(accountId);
    // Controller knows NOTHING about persistence — clean!
}
```

Testability: with the repository interface, service tests mock the repository — no database needed:

```java
@Test
void shouldCalculateSettledTotal() {
    when(tradeRepo.findSettledByAccountId("SAV2001"))
        .thenReturn(List.of(trade(100), trade(200)));

    BigDecimal total = service.calculateSettledTotal("SAV2001");

    assertThat(total).isEqualByComparingTo("300");
    // NO database! Mock returns test data. Test runs in milliseconds.
}
```

